<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Manuel Rodríguez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1771–1837</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Manuel Rodríguez was a Salvadoran independence leader who took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> uprising of 1811. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> left the Mexican Empire, he became the first head of state of El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> in 1824. His government abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the federation that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-rodriguez.jpg" alt="Juan Manuel Rodríguez" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elfather503 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Manuel_Rodr%C3%ADguez,_political_chief,_independence_leader_and_founding_father_of_Central_America.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
